WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:30.739
It's a story of poor public policy

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:33.533
and complacency going back decades.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.828
It's a story of the
inability of the Indian state

00:00:36.828 --> 00:00:39.414
to show up when the Indian
people need it the most.

00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:43.585
On Saturday, India saw the
worlds highest ever case tally

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:45.712
of over 400,000 infections.

00:00:47.005 --> 00:00:48.715
Are we likely to see a peak soon

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:51.426
or are things likely to get
worse before they get better?

00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:54.637
I don't think from the
very beginning we've ever

00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:56.890
had an accurate picture
of the virus in India

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.476
and we certainly don't have
an accurate picture now.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.283
This really was the first
tragedy that I've ever seen

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:19.746
that's played out so close to
me on my social media feeds.

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.457
People in their own
close networks of family

00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:26.377
and friends crying out for help
on their social media feeds.

00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:29.047
I mean, it can't get any closer than this.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.521
India is no stranger to pandemics

00:01:44.521 --> 00:01:46.231
but modern India has escaped some

00:01:46.231 --> 00:01:48.399
of the worst pandemics the world has seen.

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:50.401
For example, the previous version of SARS,

00:01:50.401 --> 00:01:52.320
India pretty much escaped that.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.489
So it's fair to say that India

00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:56.616
didn't really have any competence to deal

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.493
with global pandemics of this sort.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.802
India was one of the first
countries to actually

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:18.888
impose a very, very strict
nationwide lockdown.

00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:21.182
I remember Prime Minister Modi came

00:02:21.182 --> 00:02:24.435
on television at prime time
and said, within four hours

00:02:24.435 --> 00:02:27.355
the country will be locked
down effectively from midnight.

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:32.235
The urgency behind the
lockdown left many people

00:02:32.235 --> 00:02:35.029
with a lack of preparation.

00:02:35.029 --> 00:02:37.824
India has a massive internal migration,

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:40.034
waves of people really
flow from the villages.

00:02:40.034 --> 00:02:43.246
So suddenly when factories
were ordered to be shut,

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:46.457
millions of people were
rendered unemployed,

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:47.834
virtually overnight.

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.378
Then we could see waves
of these internal migrants

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:53.381
on roads trudging back to
their villages on foot.

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:56.134
There were extremely sad
and grim instances where

00:02:56.134 --> 00:02:58.011
they were so tired that
they chose to sleep

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.638
and they were down by trains and buses.

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:04.934
The predictions were for the
worst sort of carnage in India.

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:07.437
However, as the
pandemic kind of ran

00:03:07.437 --> 00:03:10.773
through the population,
India's mortality rate ended

00:03:10.773 --> 00:03:12.942
up being among the lowest in the world.

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:14.986
Everyone around could see people affected

00:03:14.986 --> 00:03:17.906
by the coronavirus, even
if they went to hospital,

00:03:17.906 --> 00:03:19.282
they would get out of hospital

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:21.201
relatively soon and relatively healthy

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:22.577
and here we are even talking

00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:25.914
about people age 70
plus, 75, 80 and older.

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:29.000
Yeah, I mean, there was a lot of theories

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.211
whether India reached a
level of herd immunity

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:32.503
at that point.

00:03:32.503 --> 00:03:33.963
They were other theories going around,

00:03:33.963 --> 00:03:35.924
like India is a warm country,

00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:38.218
whether south Asian populations

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:39.928
were more genetically immune

00:03:39.928 --> 00:03:43.765
or disposed to not feel the
worst effects of COVID, but none

00:03:43.765 --> 00:03:46.684
of them were particularly well-proven

00:03:46.684 --> 00:03:48.978
but the official numbers
were kind of showing

00:03:48.978 --> 00:03:50.813
that there was something going on anyway.

00:03:50.813 --> 00:03:53.233
You saw Modi at the World
Economic Forum saying

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:56.194
that India had basically
contained the virus

00:03:56.194 --> 00:03:59.197
and had saved the world from corona.

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:02.617
The Central Bank itself, the
reserve bank of India came out,

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:06.454
with a really quite jubilant statement

00:04:06.454 --> 00:04:10.625
in January saying that the
country had bent the virus

00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:13.419
curve like Beckham, which
was obviously a very

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.672
odd statements come
out for a Central Bank

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:18.007
but that was the kind of
atmosphere at the time.

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:23.012
You had cinemas opening, you had, you know

00:04:23.012 --> 00:04:26.224
the wedding season started and
you had these mass weddings

00:04:26.224 --> 00:04:28.476
these huge, beautiful Indian
weddings with, you know

00:04:28.476 --> 00:04:30.228
hundreds and hundreds of people.

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:33.690
And all of this was done without that very

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.109
clear public health
messaging that you need

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:37.819
when you're in the middle of a pandemic.

00:04:37.819 --> 00:04:41.489
It was almost like India
was an exceptional country

00:04:41.489 --> 00:04:42.824
that didn't succumb

00:04:42.824 --> 00:04:47.245
to a pandemic that was
affecting the rest of the world.

00:04:47.245 --> 00:04:50.456
What became clear very quickly
was what we had suspected

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.291
all along,

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:53.376
and what many epidemiologists
have been telling us

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:55.712
which is that India had
been severely under-counting

00:04:55.712 --> 00:04:58.298
both its infections and its deaths.

00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:00.049
We know that many, many, many deaths

00:05:00.049 --> 00:05:03.553
in normal times in India
aren't properly recorded

00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:05.471
particularly when you
get into the hinterland

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:09.517
which is where 70% of
India's population live.

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:11.352
Something like only 20 to 30%

00:05:11.352 --> 00:05:12.979
of deaths are properly recorded.

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:15.773
So the government engaged in a lot

00:05:15.773 --> 00:05:18.609
of grandstanding about
how fabulously it had done

00:05:18.609 --> 00:05:22.155
and yet what happened, turned
out very differently indeed.

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:37.462
This really has been the
first major global crisis

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:41.299
that's played out since
social media became such

00:05:41.299 --> 00:05:45.094
a huge factor in people's lives

00:05:45.094 --> 00:05:49.349
and particularly in India,
social media has caught on

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:52.185
in the last few years,
there are half a billion

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:53.936
WhatsApp users.

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:58.733
But what happened was
real crowdsourced chaos

00:05:58.733 --> 00:06:00.943
that was playing out on social media.

00:06:00.943 --> 00:06:05.198
There was misinformation
about fake Covid cures,

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:10.203
immunity boosters, herbal
concoctions that could save people

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:11.829
from getting Covid.

00:06:11.829 --> 00:06:15.875
Dab some lemon juice on your
nostril, and you'll be safe.

00:06:27.595 --> 00:06:29.514
The problem is that a lot

00:06:29.514 --> 00:06:32.350
of people that use
social media have gotten

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.895
onto the internet very, very recently.

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:37.230
So it's really difficult

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.608
for them to distinguish
what's fake and what's real.

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:44.487
Facebook said it had removed 12

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:47.907
million pieces of misinformation relating

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:51.369
to COVID globally, you
know, from it's network.

00:06:51.369 --> 00:06:52.912
But you know, that's really a drop

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:55.248
in the ocean considering how many millions

00:06:55.248 --> 00:06:58.084
of misinformation pieces
are still floating

00:06:58.084 --> 00:07:00.211
around cyberspace.

00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:03.297
So a lot of that
misinformation has actually

00:07:03.297 --> 00:07:08.010
come back to haunt a lot of
Indians during the pandemic.

00:07:21.732 --> 00:07:24.110
The fact is that India has over the years

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:28.948
converted itself into a
powerhouse of vaccine creation.

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:30.199
In fact, more than half

00:07:30.199 --> 00:07:32.410
of the world's children are inoculated

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.996
with a vaccine developed in India.

00:07:34.996 --> 00:07:36.998
And therefore this one particular company

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:38.708
the Serum Institute of India

00:07:38.708 --> 00:07:41.752
which is situated just
outside Mumbai was seen

00:07:41.752 --> 00:07:44.464
as a hope for the world, not just India.

00:07:45.673 --> 00:07:47.049
Adar Poonawalla is the head

00:07:47.049 --> 00:07:49.969
the CEO of the Serum Institute of India.

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:52.138
And he's this 40-year-old son

00:07:52.138 --> 00:07:56.684
of a billionaire father
who founded this company

00:07:56.684 --> 00:07:58.269
in the 1960s.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.690
In mid-2020, Poonawalla signed a deal

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:06.152
with AstraZeneca to supply one billion doses

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:08.529
of its vaccine for the world

00:08:08.529 --> 00:08:12.241
and 400 million of those
were meant to be produced

00:08:12.241 --> 00:08:14.035
by the end of the year.

00:08:14.035 --> 00:08:16.621
Now that didn't really play
out as things went ahead.

00:08:16.621 --> 00:08:19.332
By January, it transpired
that only 70 million

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:22.293
of those doses have been produced so far

00:08:22.293 --> 00:08:23.544
and in the start of January, there

00:08:23.544 --> 00:08:25.463
wasn't a huge amount of demand in India

00:08:25.463 --> 00:08:28.716
because basically there
weren't that many cases

00:08:28.716 --> 00:08:31.469
and all the while India's
government was sending tens

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:34.013
of millions of doses out overseas,

00:08:34.013 --> 00:08:37.683
it was engaged in COVID vaccine diplomacy.

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:40.478
It was really trying
to sort of needle China

00:08:40.478 --> 00:08:44.023
which was attempting to do
the same at the time as well.

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:48.319
India has exported or donated
more than 60 million doses

00:08:48.319 --> 00:08:51.489
of coronavirus vaccines to
countries far poorer than India.

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:54.700
It was seen as a massive
diplomatic victory

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.619
for India's ruling party.

00:08:56.619 --> 00:08:59.997
Little did they know
that a more potent strain

00:08:59.997 --> 00:09:01.374
of the virus was

00:09:01.374 --> 00:09:04.001
at the same time brewing
within the people in India.

00:09:18.558 --> 00:09:21.519
So there were various
consequences of India emerging

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:24.272
relatively unscathed from the first wave.

00:09:24.272 --> 00:09:27.817
One of those was the sense
to be self-sufficient.

00:09:42.957 --> 00:09:45.710
Prime Minister Modi imposed massive taxes

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:46.836
on imported goods.

00:09:48.421 --> 00:09:49.463
Unfortunately, some

00:09:49.463 --> 00:09:52.925
of these goods that were imported
was stuff that's essential

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:55.970
for our scientists,
labs to test the virus.

00:09:56.929 --> 00:09:59.849
The second mistake that
was made was the calling

00:09:59.849 --> 00:10:02.351
for elections and not just elections

00:10:02.351 --> 00:10:04.478
but massive public rallies.

00:10:04.478 --> 00:10:06.939
Some of them led by Prime
Minister Modi himself.

00:10:08.441 --> 00:10:10.735
The next rung of mistakes
that were made was

00:10:10.735 --> 00:10:14.447
giving the green light for
massive religious ceremonies.

00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:16.699
A sure shot recipe for a super-spreader.

00:10:20.453 --> 00:10:23.748
The next problem that
local authorities point

00:10:23.748 --> 00:10:26.751
out is over-centralization of powers

00:10:26.751 --> 00:10:29.003
within the Prime Minister's office

00:10:29.003 --> 00:10:33.132
including how oxygen will be
shared between the states.

00:10:34.258 --> 00:10:36.469
This really has proved
to be a game changer

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:39.055
because every time a
state fell short of oxygen

00:10:39.055 --> 00:10:41.891
it lacked the powers to go
out and get it for themselves.

00:10:53.069 --> 00:10:54.487
One of the scariest things

00:10:54.487 --> 00:10:56.197
when the second wave hit was the fact

00:10:56.197 --> 00:10:58.282
that India was running out of oxygen.

00:11:05.915 --> 00:11:10.002
I visited a private
hospital in New Delhi where

00:11:10.002 --> 00:11:12.296
they didn't know from
one day to the next, how

00:11:12.296 --> 00:11:15.508
much oxygen they were going to receive,

00:11:15.508 --> 00:11:18.302
when it would come and
how long it would last.

00:11:18.302 --> 00:11:20.554
And they had dozens of patients

00:11:20.554 --> 00:11:24.892
on oxygen and relying on
that oxygen to stay alive.

00:11:24.892 --> 00:11:27.603
Oh, I get maybe around 50 calls every day.

00:11:27.603 --> 00:11:28.813
Somebody that's asking for bed,

00:11:28.813 --> 00:11:32.233
somebody asking for cylinders,
somebody asking for drugs.

00:11:32.233 --> 00:11:35.403
We don't have anything available

00:11:35.403 --> 00:11:36.654
and patients are dying.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:41.450
Now, India produces more
oxygen than it needs.

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:42.743
The problem is that most

00:11:42.743 --> 00:11:44.870
of it is produced in the
eastern part of the country

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:47.581
which lacks supply links
to the rest of the country.

00:11:47.581 --> 00:11:50.626
On a typical day, when it's
business as usual in India

00:11:50.626 --> 00:11:54.422
hospitals will place a standing order.

00:11:54.422 --> 00:11:55.965
But when a situation breaks

00:11:55.965 --> 00:11:57.925
down the way it did with the panic

00:11:57.925 --> 00:12:02.221
of the second wave, supply
chains completely get eradicated

00:12:02.221 --> 00:12:03.764
and that is what India saw.

00:12:08.477 --> 00:12:11.772
What this led to is it
led to black marketing.

00:12:11.772 --> 00:12:13.816
The hospitals had certain
contractual agreements

00:12:13.816 --> 00:12:16.402
with oxygen suppliers,
but there are always

00:12:16.402 --> 00:12:18.362
people willing to pay more for that.

00:12:18.362 --> 00:12:19.572
As a consequence, we are

00:12:19.572 --> 00:12:22.575
in this terrible humanitarian
disaster, really,

00:12:22.575 --> 00:12:25.369
where people the citizens
of India have had to plead

00:12:25.369 --> 00:12:26.871
for oxygen on Twitter.

00:12:28.956 --> 00:12:31.792
It was an unprecedented situation, really,

00:12:31.792 --> 00:12:34.670
in India that so many

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:38.799
so-called privileged people
found themselves suddenly

00:12:38.799 --> 00:12:42.845
in the same boat, as very average Indians

00:12:42.845 --> 00:12:46.640
all fighting for that
one oxygen concentrator

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:49.310
or that one last hospital bed

00:12:49.310 --> 00:12:52.396
that one particular hospital had going.

00:12:52.396 --> 00:12:54.857
And it was really a free-for-all.

00:12:56.609 --> 00:12:58.527
While people are looking at social media

00:12:58.527 --> 00:13:00.738
hoping to find that lifeline

00:13:00.738 --> 00:13:05.034
oftentimes social media has
actually increased the trauma

00:13:05.034 --> 00:13:08.412
for families of patients
because they end up

00:13:08.412 --> 00:13:10.831
calling dozens and dozens of numbers

00:13:10.831 --> 00:13:13.918
and then find that, you know,
they've lost precious time.

00:13:13.918 --> 00:13:16.045
And there have been instances that I know

00:13:16.045 --> 00:13:18.047
of where patients have passed away

00:13:18.047 --> 00:13:21.258
because the network
hasn't quite come through.

00:13:23.302 --> 00:13:26.514
The asymmetry, I think has
been particularly stark

00:13:26.514 --> 00:13:29.308
during this crisis that
people who don't have access

00:13:29.308 --> 00:13:31.727
to the internet or don't
have a smartphone that they

00:13:31.727 --> 00:13:34.730
where they can WhatsApp
somebody to ask for something,

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:36.816
then they've been kind of really left out

00:13:36.816 --> 00:13:40.444
of this whole fight for resources.

00:13:40.444 --> 00:13:42.488
They don't even get a chance.

00:13:44.865 --> 00:13:47.284
What we were seeing is
people lying in beds.

00:13:47.284 --> 00:13:48.869
People being bundled

00:13:48.869 --> 00:13:51.455
into any possible vehicle
that was available

00:13:51.455 --> 00:13:53.541
because ambulances were in short supply

00:13:53.541 --> 00:13:56.460
and being taken to hospital
for just that instant hit

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:59.755
of oxygen that could
possibly prolong their life.

00:13:59.755 --> 00:14:03.384
What we saw was heart-rending
messages on WhatsApp

00:14:03.384 --> 00:14:06.178
where families were
telling other relatives

00:14:06.178 --> 00:14:09.306
or their friends, my loved one has died

00:14:09.306 --> 00:14:11.559
which means that the hospital
now has a bed available,

00:14:11.559 --> 00:14:12.476
you can take it.

00:14:14.478 --> 00:14:18.816
What we saw was reporters going
out on the field, going out

00:14:18.816 --> 00:14:21.277
to crematoriums and
saying that the government

00:14:21.277 --> 00:14:24.363
has told us 40 people
died in the city today.

00:14:24.363 --> 00:14:26.407
I am showing you, my camera is showing you

00:14:26.407 --> 00:14:28.701
that 500 pyres are burning right now.

00:14:29.702 --> 00:14:32.788
What we had was crematorium
workers telling us

00:14:32.788 --> 00:14:35.165
I will go to hell if I lie to you

00:14:35.165 --> 00:14:37.793
and tell you that this is
not as bad as it sounds.

00:14:44.341 --> 00:14:47.970
We went to one of the main
crematoriums on the outskirts

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:50.723
of Delhi, which is on the
banks of the Ganges River.

00:14:50.723 --> 00:14:52.558
There was so many pyres burning.

00:14:52.558 --> 00:14:54.518
The heat was extraordinary

00:14:54.518 --> 00:14:57.563
and of course, instead of
the normal mourning scenes

00:14:57.563 --> 00:15:00.190
that you would have,
all of the relatives are

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:03.944
in full PPE and no one can hear each other

00:15:03.944 --> 00:15:07.239
through the masks and over the
sound of the fires burning.

00:15:08.991 --> 00:15:11.827
It's traumatic enough to have
someone that you love die

00:15:11.827 --> 00:15:13.704
but to have to not be able to save them

00:15:13.704 --> 00:15:18.125
and to try so hard to do it
is just absolutely agonizing.

00:15:23.047 --> 00:15:26.467
So when people started asking
for vaccines urgently,

00:15:26.467 --> 00:15:28.844
India just did not have
the supply to give them.

00:15:28.844 --> 00:15:32.014
The country of 1.3 billion
people which had so generously

00:15:32.014 --> 00:15:34.850
donated vaccines to countries,
far poorer than itself

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:38.938
earlier, suddenly found
that their own population

00:15:38.938 --> 00:15:40.564
was effectively naked.

00:15:40.564 --> 00:15:44.151
It lacked the protection
that even a single dose

00:15:44.151 --> 00:15:46.070
of the coronavirus vaccine could offer.

00:15:48.405 --> 00:15:51.575
That's a big irony is that
you've got the world's

00:15:51.575 --> 00:15:54.870
largest vaccine producing hub

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:56.747
but it isn't able to supply itself

00:15:56.747 --> 00:15:58.582
let alone the rest of the world.

00:15:58.582 --> 00:16:00.125
So many of the poor

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:03.963
and middle income countries
that were dependent on India

00:16:03.963 --> 00:16:05.464
supplying the COVAX Initiative,

00:16:05.464 --> 00:16:08.425
for instance, that's just not
going to happen for a while.

00:16:08.425 --> 00:16:09.385
They're really,

00:16:09.385 --> 00:16:11.053
those countries are the
ones that are going to

00:16:11.053 --> 00:16:13.722
be very hard hit by this.

00:16:13.722 --> 00:16:15.307
And at the same time, you know

00:16:15.307 --> 00:16:17.977
I think a lot of Western
countries have been criticized

00:16:17.977 --> 00:16:19.979
for not sharing their vaccines

00:16:19.979 --> 00:16:22.314
particularly with India
and others as well.

00:16:22.314 --> 00:16:24.233
So you're really seeing
this as a, you know

00:16:24.233 --> 00:16:26.735
kind of part of the picture
of the wider sort of

00:16:26.735 --> 00:16:31.615
global inequality that we're
seeing around around vaccines.

00:16:31.615 --> 00:16:32.658
Some of the poorest

00:16:32.658 --> 00:16:36.412
and the most vulnerable
sections of society probably

00:16:36.412 --> 00:16:38.789
lack even the basic health infrastructure

00:16:38.789 --> 00:16:40.916
that is a human requirement,

00:16:40.916 --> 00:16:44.169
it's a basic right, but
probably for the first time

00:16:44.169 --> 00:16:46.547
the so-called privileged
in this country are

00:16:46.547 --> 00:16:49.508
facing what the poor
Indian faces every day.

00:16:49.508 --> 00:16:53.178
That not even money can
buy you a hospital bed

00:16:53.178 --> 00:16:54.638
and really the second wave

00:16:54.638 --> 00:16:57.349
of the coronavirus has
exposed severe cracks

00:16:57.349 --> 00:17:00.436
in Indian society and
Indian policymaking in

00:17:00.436 --> 00:17:04.231
India's healthcare system that
are essential to be filled

00:17:04.231 --> 00:17:06.191
if we have to go ahead as a nation.

00:17:11.739 --> 00:17:13.657
When cases started shooting up

00:17:13.657 --> 00:17:17.411
and there were thousands
of fatalities, a lot

00:17:17.411 --> 00:17:21.415
of people on the social, on
social media networks called

00:17:21.415 --> 00:17:23.876
for Prime Minister Modi's resignation.

00:17:23.876 --> 00:17:25.127
And then the next thing you heard was

00:17:25.127 --> 00:17:27.755
that the government had asked Facebook

00:17:27.755 --> 00:17:30.966
and Twitter to take down those posts.

00:17:30.966 --> 00:17:33.385
But there were also
instances where social media

00:17:33.385 --> 00:17:36.764
networks just took down
these hashtags on their own

00:17:36.764 --> 00:17:39.683
and then came in for a huge criticism

00:17:39.683 --> 00:17:41.977
because they were acting
really out of turn

00:17:41.977 --> 00:17:45.564
because they were actually
stifling people's criticism

00:17:45.564 --> 00:17:47.983
of the government and
of the prime minister

00:17:47.983 --> 00:17:50.194
in handling this massive tragedy.

00:17:50.194 --> 00:17:53.238
This has been the single biggest failure

00:17:53.238 --> 00:17:56.158
of the union government
in the last 73 years

00:17:56.158 --> 00:17:59.912
and 73 years is since the
independence of India.

00:17:59.912 --> 00:18:02.623
So we can see the political
impact, the political impact

00:18:02.623 --> 00:18:05.459
of Modi's popularity being eroded probably

00:18:05.459 --> 00:18:07.544
for the first time as people die

00:18:07.544 --> 00:18:11.090
as people realize that privilege
no longer protects you.

00:18:11.090 --> 00:18:12.716
Prime Minister Modi was elected

00:18:12.716 --> 00:18:15.469
on a massive majority in 2014

00:18:15.469 --> 00:18:18.013
and he repeated that in 2019,

00:18:18.013 --> 00:18:20.599
he came in on a very
business friendly platform

00:18:20.599 --> 00:18:23.811
and he has made some
considerable reforms in India

00:18:23.811 --> 00:18:26.271
like a momentous decision in 2016

00:18:26.271 --> 00:18:28.732
to demonetize part of the currency

00:18:28.732 --> 00:18:33.445
and basically ban 86% of
the currency overnight.

00:18:33.445 --> 00:18:37.157
And it didn't seem to affect
his popularity at all.

00:18:37.157 --> 00:18:41.245
So he's used to being the
leader of the country that

00:18:41.245 --> 00:18:44.498
that talks to people about
really big picture issues and

00:18:44.498 --> 00:18:48.043
and almost on, on a religious level.

00:18:48.043 --> 00:18:50.546
So I think he thought he
could float above COVID

00:18:50.546 --> 00:18:52.464
but the fact that no family

00:18:52.464 --> 00:18:55.300
in India has been
untouched by this epidemic.

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:58.595
Everybody knows somebody who's
who's sick or who's died.

00:19:00.222 --> 00:19:00.556
You know, there's no way
that the government can hide

00:19:00.556 --> 00:19:02.558
You know, there's no way
that the government can hide

00:19:02.558 --> 00:19:04.059
from the impact of this virus.

00:19:04.059 --> 00:19:06.186
And as, as one analyst said to us

00:19:06.186 --> 00:19:08.438
you can explain away bad GDP numbers

00:19:08.438 --> 00:19:11.650
but you can't explain
away a dead family member.

00:19:11.650 --> 00:19:14.653
That's not something that
the government is going to

00:19:14.653 --> 00:19:16.113
be able to cover up.

00:19:16.905 --> 00:19:20.868
It's unclear yet though,
whether his support has really

00:19:20.868 --> 00:19:24.705
been dented in the long
term, elections are still

00:19:24.705 --> 00:19:26.915
years away.

00:19:26.915 --> 00:19:29.251
So it's really yet to be seen
if this is a turning point

00:19:29.251 --> 00:19:34.006
in Modi's support and
his political narrative

00:19:34.006 --> 00:19:35.799
whether he can come back from this.

00:20:05.913 --> 00:20:09.333
B.1.617, the variant of
the novel Coronavirus,

00:20:09.333 --> 00:20:13.503
first sequenced in India is
fast-spreading across the world.

00:20:13.503 --> 00:20:17.966
If Indians are not vaccinated
quickly, if India doesn't

00:20:17.966 --> 00:20:20.928
do everything to contain
its spread quickly enough,

00:20:20.928 --> 00:20:24.139
the risk is that more and
more of these mutations

00:20:24.139 --> 00:20:26.516
could threaten not just
the health and livelihoods

00:20:26.516 --> 00:20:27.935
of the rest of the world,

00:20:27.935 --> 00:20:32.648
but also immense economic supply chains.

00:20:32.648 --> 00:20:36.360
India contributes more than
8% to global growth, but

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:39.363
if you integrate what
India does into the systems

00:20:39.363 --> 00:20:41.531
of the world, into large banks,

00:20:41.531 --> 00:20:43.575
into large pharmaceutical companies, most

00:20:43.575 --> 00:20:46.745
of the seafaring ships, which
haul cargo from one country

00:20:46.745 --> 00:20:49.748
to the other, most of these
ships are manned by Indians.

00:20:49.748 --> 00:20:52.376
What will happen to the
supply chains around the world

00:20:52.376 --> 00:20:55.545
if Indians are unable to get
up, get out of their beds,

00:20:55.545 --> 00:20:57.589
go to work, be healthy,

00:20:57.589 --> 00:20:59.883
think, be on their feet,

00:20:59.883 --> 00:21:03.011
enable that business continues as usual?

00:21:04.763 --> 00:21:07.182
As India's power and stature has grown

00:21:07.182 --> 00:21:09.226
over the years, as it has taken

00:21:09.226 --> 00:21:13.188
on more and more leadership
roles on the global stage.

00:21:13.188 --> 00:21:16.942
World powers in particular,
the U.S. is very reliant

00:21:16.942 --> 00:21:20.737
on India as providing a kind
of buffer against the rise

00:21:20.737 --> 00:21:24.658
of China in particular, in the Asian region.

00:21:24.658 --> 00:21:26.285
It's really important that

00:21:26.285 --> 00:21:29.454
as a democratic country
on the border with China

00:21:29.454 --> 00:21:32.499
India maintains a strong global role

00:21:32.499 --> 00:21:35.544
and if it's unable to get
this pandemic under control

00:21:35.544 --> 00:21:38.338
if it's unable to boost
its health infrastructure

00:21:38.338 --> 00:21:42.259
enough to create a stable
environment and therefore

00:21:42.259 --> 00:21:46.513
allow its economy to recover from the lockdowns,

00:21:46.513 --> 00:21:50.392
then that global stature that India
has, will be under threat.

00:21:51.893 --> 00:21:54.146
It's a lesson, not just
for policymakers in India.

00:21:54.146 --> 00:21:56.857
It's a lesson for leaders everywhere

00:21:56.857 --> 00:22:00.652
about the areas of
policymaking that need focus.

00:22:00.652 --> 00:22:04.239
About what could go wrong
with just one single decision.

00:22:04.239 --> 00:22:07.701
That a tiny virus, unseen
by the naked eye can take

00:22:07.701 --> 00:22:09.202
down strong men all over the world,

00:22:09.202 --> 00:22:10.537
if they're not careful.

